#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125443} :activity/id #uuid "e3965249-6bb4-5bb3-b83d-3689a8e9f9e5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049585 :topic/id #uuid "024170b2-c2d1-503c-b046-b4baf9077735" :topic/math-academy-id 1911} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125443 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125800 :tutorial/content "We can use repeated matrix multiplication to determine the state of a system given its initial state and some information about how one state transitions to the next state.\n\nTo explain this concretely, suppose we have a population where people live either in the city or the countryside. A study carried out by the local authority shows that, each year,\n\n- $6\\%$ of the city population relocates to the countryside,\n\n- $3\\%$ of the countryside population relocates to the city, and\n\n- everyone else remains where they are.\n\nThis information can be represented using a diagram, like the one below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab6b3cecac2af22b8665078db56c7daf492c0381434c9c88fe21f2630f743466.png)\n\nWe can also present this information as a table:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **City** | **Countryside** |\n| To: | **City** | $0.94$ | $0.03$ |\n| **Countryside** | $0.06$ | $0.97$ |  |\n\nWe want to use this information to determine how the population will be distributed $n$ years after the study was carried out. \n\nFirst, let's turn this table into a matrix $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this matrix has the following properties:\n\n- it is a square matrix\n\n- the sum of the entries in each column equals $1$\n\n- all entries in the matrix are non-negative\n\nA square matrix with these properties is called a **stochastic matrix**.\n\nNow suppose that, at the time of the study, $70\\%$ of the population lived in the city and $30\\%$ in the countryside. We can represent this **initial state** using a so-called **stochastic vector** $\\mathbf x_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.70 \\\\ 0.30\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStochastic vectors contain non-negative entries only, and the sum of the entries equals $1.$ Note that each column of $P$ is a stochastic vector.\n\nNow that we have our stochastic matrix and initial state vector, we can use matrix multiplication to calculate the population distribution sometime after the initial study took place:\n\n- To calculate the population distribution after one year, we calculate the product $P\\mathbf x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe resulting vector \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.667 \\\\ 0.333\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ntells us that one year after the study, $66.7$% of the population will live in the city, and $33.3$% will live in the countryside.\n\n- To model the distribution after $2$ years, we compute $P\\mathbf x_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe resulting vector \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.637 \\\\ 0.363\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ntells us that two years after the study, $63.7$% of the population will live in the city, and $36.3$% will live in the countryside." :tutorial/id #uuid "0d87aed7-76da-5126-8eee-f38ced9a46f9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3645 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4f00b242-2400-5948-9531-618af1db558d" :step/math-academy-id 20288 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125444 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124544 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124353 :question/id #uuid "e8f30d47-bfd2-50ea-b2ad-d474e2a58d29" :question/math-academy-id "e-5598"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbdd778-7ace-5539-9d6f-cdf01804b237" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402468}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix Given Some Data"} :step/id #uuid "2ccc7d00-22a8-5d1a-a0d0-a3e6b10db6f5" :step/math-academy-id 20289 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125445}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125445 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125801 :tutorial/content "Let's reconsider the population we looked at earlier, where the stochastic vector that represents the initial distribution of people between the city and the countryside is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.70 \\\\ 0.30\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand the yearly change in this distribution is represented by the stochastic matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEarlier, we found that the population distribution after $1$ and $2$ years is given by the vectors $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2,$ \n respectively, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{x}_0, \\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots$ are called **state vectors**, and $\\mathbf x_0$ is the **initial state**. \n\nTo find the state vector corresponding to the $(k+1)$th year, we left-multiply the state vector corresponding to the $k$th year by $P.$ This gives rise to the following sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sequence of stochastic vectors $\\mathbf{x}_0, \\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots$ related to a stochastic matrix $P$ in this way is called a **Markov chain**." :tutorial/id #uuid "f6720cd1-1b8b-5b03-9092-c29474fc606d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3646 :tutorial/title "Markov Chains"} :step/id #uuid "730b29bf-923b-5d88-9f79-57bed7ca1ca8" :step/math-academy-id 20290 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125457}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125446 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124545 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124354 :question/id #uuid "95a45716-8c29-5955-af3c-43b6344912da" :question/math-academy-id "e-5599"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e93dde4-a8e8-548a-801e-3d9a320a4513" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378664}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix and Making a Prediction"} :step/id #uuid "bf64f3e5-2318-517c-9967-386760b0f779" :step/math-academy-id 20291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125457 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124554 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124363 :question/id #uuid "09540ff7-0444-5169-b582-471be2f1972d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5971"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4821de45-1b6b-5b4f-be0e-96cf5395bc8d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391917}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a State Vector"} :step/id #uuid "c314ef44-f421-5bc5-b410-ab965ccc6457" :step/math-academy-id 22210 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125446}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124614 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124425 :question/id #uuid "25622a0e-3e5b-5f09-bef6-afaa30d153d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9162"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66b24a98-c641-5e08-8bec-30f221d1bb22" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378671}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Stochastic Vectors and Stochastic Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "d7e8a13c-5f02-5712-9c41-daa1892a5092" :step/math-academy-id 34164 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125444}}] :activity/title "Markov Chains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124711}]}
